(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-07"] = {"chem-12-07-m01":{"idea":"Group names come from properties of the elements. Group 16 is named after its link with ores.","steps":["Group 16 contains O, S, Se, Te and Po.","Many metal ores are oxides or sulfides of these elements.","The Greek-based name chalcogens means ore formers.","So group 16 is the chalcogens."],"others":"Alkali metals are group 1 and the name has nothing to do with this group. Noble gases are group 18. Halogens, meaning salt formers, are group 17.","trap":"Group 16 chalcogens, group 17 halogens, group 18 noble gases.","answer":"chalcogens, which means ore formers"},"chem-12-07-m02":{"idea":"Outer configuration comes from the number of valence electrons.","steps":["Halogens are in group 17, with 7 valence electrons.","Two of them are in the s subshell: ns².","The other five are in the p subshell: np⁵.","So the configuration is ns²np⁵. One more electron makes ns²np⁶."],"others":"ns²np⁴ is group 16. ns²np⁶ is a noble gas with a full shell. ns²np³ is group 15.","trap":"p-electron count equals group number minus 12.","answer":"ns²np⁵"},"chem-12-07-m03":{"idea":"Electronegativity rises across a period and falls down a group.","steps":["The trend is highest at the top right of the table.","Noble gases are left out, since they do not usually bond.","That puts fluorine at the top of group 17.","So fluorine is the most electronegative element."],"others":"Oxygen is second in electronegativity. Nitrogen is lower than oxygen. Chlorine is in the same group as fluorine but lower down, so it is smaller in value.","trap":"Fluorine is the most electronegative element at 4.0 on the Pauling scale.","answer":"fluorine"},"chem-12-07-m04":{"idea":"Electron gain enthalpy does not follow size alone. Very small atoms have a crowding effect.","steps":["Gain enthalpy rises in magnitude from I to Br to Cl.","Fluorine is very small, so the 2p electrons are packed close together.","The new electron is repelled by these crowded electrons.","So fluorine releases less energy than chlorine, and chlorine is most negative."],"others":"Bromine is not the largest of the four, and size does not give the highest value. Iodine has low ionisation enthalpy, which is unrelated to gaining an electron. Fluorine being the smallest is the reason it falls short.","trap":"Expect fluorine to be top, but it is not. Chlorine wins.","answer":"chlorine, not fluorine"},"chem-12-07-m05":{"idea":"The physical state depends on the size of the molecule and the forces between molecules.","steps":["Oxygen forms strong double bonds, so it exists as small O₂ molecules.","Small molecules have weak forces between them, so oxygen is a gas.","Sulfur makes single bonds, so it forms S₈ rings.","Large S₈ molecules have much stronger van der Waals forces, so sulfur is a solid."],"others":"Sulfur does not form hydrogen bonds with itself. Oxygen does not exist as O₃ in air, and sulfur is not S₂ at room temperature. Atomic mass is not the reason, since oxygen is lighter, not heavier, than sulfur.","trap":"Strong intramolecular bonds in O₂ do not mean strong forces between molecules.","answer":"oxygen exists as small O₂ molecules, while sulfur exists as large S₈ rings"},"chem-12-07-m06":{"idea":"Hydrogen bonding raises boiling points.","steps":["Oxygen is small and very electronegative.","So water molecules form O–H···O hydrogen bonds.","These bonds hold the molecules together as a liquid.","Sulfur is less electronegative, so H₂S has only weak forces and is a gas."],"others":"H₂S has the higher molecular mass, so that cannot be the reason. H₂S does not form strong hydrogen bonds. Sulfur is less electronegative than oxygen, not more.","trap":"Heavier does not always mean higher boiling point. Check for hydrogen bonds.","answer":"water molecules are linked by hydrogen bonds"},"chem-12-07-m07":{"idea":"Acid strength of H–X depends on how easily H⁺ leaves.","steps":["Down group 17, the halogen atom gets larger.","The H–X bond gets longer and weaker.","A weaker bond loses H⁺ more easily.","So acidity rises: HF < HCl < HBr < HI."],"others":"Each of the other orders puts the halides in a wrong sequence. HF is the weakest acid, not HI. HI is the strongest, not HF or HCl.","trap":"Bond strength matters here, not electronegativity.","answer":"HF < HCl < HBr < HI"},"chem-12-07-m08":{"idea":"Fluorine cannot go above −1 in compounds.","steps":["Fluorine is the most electronegative element.","It has no d orbitals, so it cannot expand its octet.","So it only takes one electron, to give −1.","Other halogens can show +1, +3, +5 and +7 as well."],"others":"A list with +1, +3 or +5 describes chlorine, bromine or iodine in compounds with oxygen or other halogens. Fluorine has no positive states because nothing is more electronegative than it.","trap":"Fluorine: only −1 (and 0 as the element).","answer":"−1 only"},"chem-12-07-m09":{"idea":"Oxidation states in a neutral compound add up to zero.","steps":["Let the oxidation state of S be x.","H is +1 and O is −2.","2(+1) + 2x + 7(−2) = 0.","2 + 2x − 14 = 0, so 2x = 12 and x = +6."],"others":"The values +7, +4 and +2 do not satisfy the sum-to-zero rule. +7 is too high for sulfur, whose maximum is +6.","trap":"Check: the sum is 2 + 12 − 14 = 0.","answer":"+6"},"chem-12-07-m10":{"idea":"Allotropes are different forms of the same element.","steps":["Oxygen has two allotropes.","One is dioxygen, O₂, in the air.","The other is ozone, O₃, made by a silent electric discharge in oxygen.","So the two are O₂ and O₃."],"others":"Atomic O is not a stable allotrope. S₈ is a form of sulfur, not oxygen. O₄ is not a normal form of oxygen.","trap":"Allotropes use the same element only.","answer":"dioxygen, O₂, and ozone, O₃"},"chem-12-07-m11":{"idea":"Transition temperature is the point where two forms are in equilibrium.","steps":["Sulfur has rhombic and monoclinic forms.","Rhombic is stable below the transition temperature.","Monoclinic is stable above it.","This temperature is about 369 K (96 °C)."],"others":"298 K is room temperature, where rhombic sulfur is already stable. 273 K is the freezing point of water. 393 K is above the transition and near sulfur's melting range.","trap":"Remember 369 K, about 96 °C.","answer":"369 K"},"chem-12-07-m12":{"idea":"More oxygen on chlorine gives a more stable anion and a stronger acid.","steps":["The acids are HOCl, HClO₂, HClO₃ and HClO₄.","More oxygen atoms pull electron density from the O–H bond.","The negative charge on the anion is shared over more oxygen atoms.","So HClO₄ loses H⁺ most easily and is strongest."],"others":"HClO₂, HClO₃ and HOCl have fewer oxygen atoms. They spread the charge over fewer atoms, so each is weaker. The order is HOCl < HClO₂ < HClO₃ < HClO₄.","trap":"Count the oxygen atoms: more means stronger.","answer":"HClO₄"},"chem-12-07-m13":{"idea":"A catalyst speeds up a reaction and is unchanged at the end.","steps":["Heating KClO₃ alone needs a high temperature.","With MnO₂, the reaction 2KClO₃ → 2KCl + 3O₂ goes at a lower temperature.","MnO₂ is recovered unchanged after the reaction.","So MnO₂ is a catalyst."],"others":"MnO₂ does not remove water, so it is not a dehydrating agent. It is not oxidised to MnO₄⁻, and it is not reduced to Mn. It stays as MnO₂.","trap":"Something recovered unchanged is a catalyst.","answer":"a catalyst, which lowers the temperature needed"},"chem-12-07-m14":{"idea":"An amphoteric oxide reacts with both acids and bases.","steps":["Al₂O₃ reacts with HCl, giving a salt and water.","It also reacts with NaOH, giving an aluminate.","Reacting with both makes it amphoteric.","Na₂O is basic, SO₂ is acidic, and CO is neutral."],"others":"CO is neutral, so it reacts with neither. Na₂O is a basic oxide and reacts with acids. SO₂ is acidic and reacts with bases. None of them reacts with both.","trap":"Amphoteric: both acid and base. Think of Al₂O₃ and ZnO.","answer":"Al₂O₃, which reacts with both acids and bases"},"chem-12-07-m15":{"idea":"The contact process uses a solid catalyst for the oxidation of SO₂.","steps":["The reaction is 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃.","The catalyst is vanadium(V) oxide, V₂O₅.","It is cheaper than platinum and is not poisoned as easily.","Conditions are about 700 K and 1–2 bar."],"others":"Nickel is used in hydrogenation. Copper is used in the oxidation of methanol. Iron is the catalyst in the Haber process for ammonia.","trap":"Contact process: V₂O₅. Haber process: Fe.","answer":"vanadium(V) oxide, V₂O₅"},"chem-12-07-m16":{"idea":"The way SO₃ is absorbed is chosen to avoid a mist.","steps":["SO₃ reacts with water very strongly and gives out a lot of heat.","The heat turns the acid into a fine mist that is hard to condense.","So SO₃ is dissolved in concentrated H₂SO₄ instead.","This gives oleum, H₂S₂O₇, which is then diluted with water."],"others":"The product with water is H₂SO₄, not sulfurous acid. The reaction gives out a lot of heat, not none. Water does react with SO₃, which is the problem.","trap":"Absorbing in water directly is a heat and mist problem.","answer":"water would give a fine mist of acid droplets which is hard to condense"},"chem-12-07-m17":{"idea":"Bleaching can be by reduction or by oxidation, and the two behave differently.","steps":["SO₂ reduces the coloured dye to a colourless form.","In air, oxygen slowly oxidises the dye back.","So the colour returns after some time.","Chlorine, by contrast, bleaches by oxidation and the change is permanent."],"others":"Oxidation is how chlorine bleaches, and its effect is permanent. SO₂ does not act by hydrolysis. Adsorption is not the mechanism for SO₂.","trap":"SO₂ reduces and the bleaching is temporary. Cl₂ oxidises and it is permanent.","answer":"reduction, and the colour slowly returns in air"},"chem-12-07-m18":{"idea":"Interhalogens have a pattern in their formulae.","steps":["Interhalogens have the forms XX′, XX′₃, XX′₅ and XX′₇.","The number of smaller atoms is odd: 1, 3, 5 or 7.","ClF₃, BrF₅ and IF₇ fit this pattern.","ClF₂ has two atoms of F, which is even, so it does not exist."],"others":"IF₇ is a known XX′₇ compound. ClF₃ is a known XX′₃ compound. BrF₅ is a known XX′₅ compound.","trap":"Odd number of smaller atoms: 1, 3, 5, 7.","answer":"ClF₂"},"chem-12-07-m19":{"idea":"The shape follows from the number of electron pairs on the central atom.","steps":["In IF₇, iodine forms 7 bonds with fluorine.","It has no lone pair.","Seven pairs around one atom take a pentagonal bipyramid shape.","Five F atoms lie in a plane, with one above and one below."],"others":"A trigonal bipyramid has only five positions. An octahedron has six positions. A square pyramid has five bonds. None of these fits seven bond pairs.","trap":"Count the pairs: 7 means pentagonal bipyramidal.","answer":"pentagonal bipyramidal"},"chem-12-07-m20":{"idea":"Use bond pairs and lone pairs to find the shape.","steps":["Xe has 8 valence electrons. Four bond with F.","That leaves 4 electrons, which is 2 lone pairs.","Six electron pairs point to an octahedron.","The two lone pairs sit opposite each other, so the four F atoms form a flat square."],"others":"See-saw has one lone pair on four bonds. Square pyramidal has one lone pair with five bonds. Tetrahedral would have no lone pairs on four bonds.","trap":"Two lone pairs on opposite sides: square planar.","answer":"square planar"},"chem-12-07-m21":{"idea":"Use bond pairs and lone pairs to find the shape.","steps":["Xe has 8 valence electrons. Three are used to form bonds with O.","One lone pair remains on Xe.","Four electron pairs point to a tetrahedron.","One corner holds the lone pair, so the atoms form a pyramid."],"others":"Tetrahedral shape is for four bond pairs and no lone pair. Planar triangular would need no lone pair. Linear needs two atoms only.","trap":"Three bonds plus one lone pair: pyramidal, like NH₃.","answer":"pyramidal"},"chem-12-07-m22":{"idea":"Complete hydrolysis keeps xenon in the same oxidation state.","steps":["Write the reaction: XeF₆ + 3H₂O → XeO₃ + 6HF.","Xenon stays at +6.","Fluorine leaves as hydrogen fluoride.","Partial hydrolysis gives XeOF₄ instead."],"others":"XeF₂ and O₂ would need a change in the oxidation state of xenon. Xe and HF would need xenon to be reduced. XeO₂ is not a normal hydrolysis product here.","trap":"Complete hydrolysis gives XeO₃. Partial gives XeOF₄.","answer":"XeO₃ and HF"},"chem-12-07-m23":{"idea":"Only a noble gas that loses electron density easily can react.","steps":["Ionisation enthalpy falls down group 18.","Xenon has a lower ionisation enthalpy than helium and neon.","So it can share electrons with very electronegative elements.","Fluorine and oxygen are such elements, so xenon forms compounds with them."],"others":"Xenon has a complete outer shell, like all noble gases. Helium and neon are not radioactive. Xenon has a lower, not higher, ionisation enthalpy than helium and neon.","trap":"Heavier noble gases are easier to ionise.","answer":"xenon has a lower ionisation enthalpy, and fluorine and oxygen are the most electronegative elements"},"chem-12-07-m24":{"idea":"Covalency above the octet needs vacant d orbitals.","steps":["Sulfur is in period 3 and has vacant 3d orbitals.","It can promote electrons there and form six bonds, as in SF₆.","Oxygen is in period 2, with only 2s and 2p orbitals.","So oxygen has no d orbitals and its covalency is limited to four."],"others":"Oxygen is smaller, not larger, than sulfur. Oxygen is more electronegative, not less. Sulfur has the same number of outer electrons as oxygen, six.","trap":"Period 2 elements cannot expand their octet.","answer":"oxygen has no vacant d orbitals in its valence shell"},"chem-12-07-m25":{"idea":"Helium has only two electrons.","steps":["Helium has atomic number 2.","Both electrons go in the 1s orbital: 1s².","The first shell is then full.","Other noble gases have ns²np⁶."],"others":"Xenon, neon and argon all have an ns²np⁶ outer shell, not 1s². Only helium has just one shell.","trap":"Helium is the one noble gas without an octet.","answer":"helium"},"chem-12-07-m26":{"idea":"Reducing power depends on how easily the hydride gives up hydrogen.","steps":["Going down group 16 the X–H bond gets longer and weaker.","A weaker bond lets the hydride give up hydrogen more easily.","So reducing power rises from H₂O to H₂Te.","H₂Te is at the end of the series, so it is the strongest reducing agent."],"others":"H₂O has the strongest bond and the weakest reducing power. H₂S and H₂Se are in the middle, so each is weaker than H₂Te.","trap":"Down the group: weaker X–H bond, stronger reducing agent.","answer":"H₂Te"},"chem-12-07-n01":{"idea":"Use the mole ratio from the balanced equation.","steps":["n(KClO₃) = mass ÷ molar mass = 12.25/122.5 = 0.1 mol.","2 mol KClO₃ give 3 mol O₂.","So 0.1 mol gives 0.1 × 3/2 = 0.15 mol O₂.","Mass of O₂ = 0.15 × 32 = 4.8 g."],"trap":"Check: oxygen is 48 of the 122.5 g in each mole of KClO₃, which is 39.2%, and 39.2% of 12.25 g is 4.8 g.","answer":"4.8 g"},"chem-12-07-n02":{"idea":"Find moles of O₂ from the ratio, then multiply by the molar volume.","steps":["n(KClO₃) = 24.5/122.5 = 0.2 mol.","O₂ formed = 0.2 × 3/2 = 0.3 mol.","Volume = 0.3 × 22.4 L.","Volume = 6.72 L."],"trap":"Check: this is twice the amount in the previous problem, scaled by volume.","answer":"6.72 L"},"chem-12-07-n03":{"idea":"The oxidation numbers in a neutral compound add up to zero.","steps":["Na₂S₄O₆ has 2 Na, 4 S and 6 O.","Let the average oxidation number of S be x.","2(+1) + 4x + 6(−2) = 0.","4x = 10, so x = +2.5."],"trap":"This is an average. The real values are +5, 0, 0, +5.","answer":"+2.5"},"chem-12-07-n04":{"idea":"Sulfur and SO₂ are in a 1 : 1 mole ratio.","steps":["n(S) = 16/32 = 0.5 mol.","S + O₂ → SO₂, so 0.5 mol S gives 0.5 mol SO₂.","Molar mass of SO₂ = 32 + 32 = 64 g/mol.","Mass = 0.5 × 64 = 32 g."],"trap":"Check: mass of S plus oxygen taken in is 16 + 16 = 32 g.","answer":"32 g"},"chem-12-07-n05":{"idea":"The SO₂ to SO₃ ratio is 1 : 1.","steps":["n(SO₂) = 64/64 = 1 mol.","2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃, so 1 mol SO₂ gives 1 mol SO₃.","Molar mass of SO₃ = 32 + 48 = 80 g/mol.","Mass = 1 × 80 = 80 g."],"trap":"Check: 64 g of SO₂ plus 16 g of oxygen gives 80 g.","answer":"80 g"},"chem-12-07-n06":{"idea":"Molarity is moles of solute per litre of solution. Find the mass of H₂SO₄ in one litre.","steps":["Mass of 1 L solution = 1000 mL × 1.84 g/mL = 1840 g.","Mass of H₂SO₄ = 1840 × 0.98 = 1803.2 g.","Moles = 1803.2/98 = 18.4 mol.","Molarity = 18.4 mol per 1 L = 18.4 M."],"trap":"Check: 100 g of solution holds 98 g, which is 1 mol, in 54.35 mL, so about 18.4 M.","answer":"18.4 M"},"chem-12-07-n07":{"idea":"Percentage by mass is the part's mass divided by the total, times 100.","steps":["Molar mass of XeF₂ = 131 + 2 × 19 = 169 g/mol.","Xe percentage = (131/169) × 100.","131/169 = 0.7751.","So the percentage is 77.5%."],"trap":"Check: F is 38/169 = 22.5%, and 100 − 22.5 = 77.5.","answer":"77.5%"},"chem-12-07-n08":{"idea":"Find each oxidation number of Cl, then add them.","steps":["For HClOₙ, with H = +1 and O = −2: +1 + x − 2n = 0.","So x = 2n − 1.","n = 1, 2, 3, 4 gives +1, +3, +5, +7.","Sum = 1 + 3 + 5 + 7 = 16."],"trap":"Check each: HOCl +1, HClO₂ +3, HClO₃ +5, HClO₄ +7.","answer":"16"},"chem-12-07-n09":{"idea":"Count electron pairs on xenon.","steps":["Xe has 8 valence electrons.","Each of the 6 F atoms brings 1 electron: 8 + 6 = 14.","Pairs = 14/2 = 7.","6 are bond pairs, so 1 lone pair remains."],"trap":"Check: 7 pairs give a distorted octahedral shape, in which the lone pair sits at one position.","answer":"1"},"chem-12-07-n10":{"idea":"Use the mole ratio of ozone to oxygen.","steps":["n(O₃) = 48/48 = 1 mol.","2O₃ → 3O₂, so 1 mol O₃ gives 1.5 mol O₂.","Volume = 1.5 × 22.4 L.","Volume = 33.6 L."],"trap":"Check: 48 g of oxygen atoms is 3 mol of atoms, which makes 1.5 mol of O₂.","answer":"33.6 L"},"chem-12-07-n11":{"idea":"The mole ratio of MnO₂ to Cl₂ is 1 : 1.","steps":["n(MnO₂) = 87/87 = 1 mol.","MnO₂ + 4HCl → MnCl₂ + Cl₂ + 2H₂O, so 1 mol gives 1 mol Cl₂.","Molar mass of Cl₂ = 71 g/mol.","Mass = 1 × 71 = 71 g."],"trap":"Check by electrons: Mn goes from +4 to +2, taking 2 electrons. One Cl₂ gives out 2 electrons, so the ratio is 1 : 1.","answer":"71 g"}};
